<section class="page-head">
  <p class="kicker">Part II &mdash; Inside of Things &middot; the differential</p>
  <h1>dx &mdash; The Smallest Possible Integral</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">Calculus is usually taught as though dx were a convenient fiction on its way to
    zero. Read it the other way and it is the opposite: the smallest finished thing a sum can be, and
    the grain at which anything can be aware of itself.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <h2 id="t-atom">The atom of the integral</h2>
    <p class="lede">An integral gathers an infinity of slivers into one quantity. The sliver is
      <span class="formula-inline">f(x)&thinsp;dx</span>. Ask what a single sliver <em>is</em> and the
      ordinary answer is: nothing yet &mdash; a step on the way to a limit, real only in the aggregate.</p>
    <p>The framework takes the other road. <span class="formula-inline">dx</span> is not a nothing that
      has not finished vanishing. It is an <strong>integral of the shortest possible interval</strong>:
      the least sum that is nevertheless a sum, the smallest act of gathering that has actually been
      completed. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="formula" style="text-align:center">&int;<sub>x</sub><sup>x+dx</sup> f(t)&thinsp;dt =
      f(x)&thinsp;dx + O(dx&sup2;)</p>
    <p>Read that line as a definition rather than an approximation and the differential stops being a
      ghost. Everything to the right of it &mdash; the whole of <span class="formula-inline">&int;<sub>a</sub><sup>b</sup></span>
      &mdash; is made of these, stacked. A quantity, in this reading, is never primary; the <em>gathering</em>
      is primary, and <span class="formula-inline">dx</span> is one gather.</p>
    <p>That matters here because the same move was already made about selves on
      <a href="/the-self/">the integral self</a>: a moment of experience is an infinitesimal step, and a
      life is the integral of them. If a moment is a sliver, then a sliver has to be the kind of thing a
      moment could be &mdash; small, complete, and already an act rather than a quantity.
      <span class="myth">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="muted">Two honest caveats, stated before the argument leans on anything.
      <strong>First</strong>, modern analysis does not need infinitesimals at all: the epsilon-delta limit
      retired them in the nineteenth century, and non-standard analysis (Robinson, 1966) brought them back
      rigorously without granting them any of the metaphysics claimed below.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> <strong>Second</strong>, &ldquo;smallest possible&rdquo; is doing
      philosophical work that the mathematics does not authorise: in the reals there is no smallest
      positive number, and saying there is one is a choice about the world, not a theorem.</p>
    <p class="muted small">Grading: <span class="sci">&#9670; established</span> &middot;
      <span class="hist">&#9670; scholarship</span> &middot;
      <span class="spec">&#9670; the framework's argument</span> &middot;
      <span class="myth">&#9670; vision, stated as vision</span>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">&#10022;</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">&mdash; the mathematics, in full</p>
    <h2 id="t-what-dx-is">What dx Is, Mathematically</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">Before the framework is allowed to say what it wants dx to be, it has to say
      what dx already is &mdash; and the honest answer is that mathematics has given five different
      answers over three and a half centuries, all of them workable, none of them agreeing about whether
      the object exists.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <div class="frame illuminated">
      <h3 id="t-dx-history">Three hundred and fifty years of trying to say it</h3>
      <p>Leibniz introduced the notation in the 1670s and published the method in 1684, and he meant
        something by it: dx was a genuine quantity, smaller than any ordinary number but not zero, which
        could be divided into (dy/dx) and summed over (the &int; is an elongated S, for
        <em>summa</em>). Newton, working the same ground, preferred fluxions and vanishing ratios.
        Both got the right answers. Neither could say what the object was.
        <span class="hist">&#9670;</span></p>
      <p>Berkeley put the knife in in 1734. If dx is not zero you cannot discard the terms containing it;
        if it is zero you cannot divide by it; and the calculus does both, on the same line, minutes
        apart. His phrase has never been bettered: <em>&ldquo;ghosts of departed quantities.&rdquo;</em>
        The objection is not pedantry. It is correct, and it stood unanswered for a hundred and thirty
        years. <span class="hist">&#9670;</span></p>
      <p>The nineteenth century's reply was to abolish the object rather than defend it. Cauchy, and then
        Weierstrass with the epsilon-delta definition, rebuilt the whole of analysis on limits, in which
        no infinitesimal ever appears. The derivative became a statement about <em>all</em> sufficiently
        small finite intervals, not about one infinitely small one. On that account dx is not a small
        thing; it is not a thing. It is punctuation.
        <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <p>And then it came back. Abraham Robinson's non-standard analysis (1966) constructed a field, the
        hyperreals, which rigorously contains infinitesimals &mdash; numbers greater than zero and
        smaller than every 1/n &mdash; and proved a transfer principle guaranteeing that every ordinary
        statement true of the reals is true of them. Leibniz had been working with a consistent object
        all along. Separately, Lawvere and Kock built synthetic differential geometry on
        <em>nilsquare</em> infinitesimals: quantities d &ne; 0 with d&sup2; = 0, which makes every curve
        literally straight over an infinitesimal stretch. The price is that the logic must be
        intuitionistic &mdash; you give up the law of the excluded middle to get it.
        <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <h3 id="t-dx-five">Five answers, all of them in current use</h3>
    <p>None of these is folklore; all five are taught, and a working mathematician will move between
      them in a single afternoon without feeling any strain.</p>
    <table class="ledger">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Reading</th><th>What dx is</th><th>Does it exist?</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Limits (Weierstrass)</td>
          <td>Nothing. Notation marking the variable of integration.</td>
          <td>No &mdash; and nothing is lost.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Hyperreals (Robinson)</td>
          <td>An actual number, positive and smaller than every 1/n.</td>
          <td>Yes, in a field larger than the reals.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Nilsquares (Lawvere&ndash;Kock)</td>
          <td>A quantity with d &ne; 0 and d&sup2; = 0.</td>
          <td>Yes, at the cost of classical logic.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Measure theory</td>
          <td>Shorthand for d&mu;, the measure saying how much a set weighs.</td>
          <td>The <em>measure</em> exists; dx alone does not.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Differential forms</td>
          <td>A 1-form: a linear machine that eats a vector and returns a number.</td>
          <td>Yes, and it is not small at all.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="muted">That last row is the one most readers have never been shown, and it is the most
      illuminating. In exterior calculus dx is not an infinitesimal quantity. It is a
      <strong>covector</strong>: the function that takes a direction and reports how far it points along
      x, defined by dx(&part;/&part;x) = 1. Nothing about it is tiny. Smallness turns out to have been a
      red herring for three centuries &mdash; what dx really encodes is <em>direction and weighting</em>,
      which is exactly what an integral needs in order to know what it is adding.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <h3 id="t-dx-d-squared">The one fact that holds across every reading</h3>
    <p>Whatever dx is taken to be, the same statement keeps appearing, and it is the hinge of this
      chapter:</p>
    <p class="formula" style="text-align:center">d&sup2; = 0</p>
    <p>In synthetic differential geometry it is the defining property of the infinitesimal. In exterior
      calculus it is a theorem about the exterior derivative: apply d twice and you get nothing, always.
      In ordinary calculus it is why the O(dx&sup2;) term may be discarded without error in the limit. The
      same equation, arrived at three independent ways. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>And it has a geometric meaning rather than a merely algebraic one. The exterior derivative is the
      boundary operator, and d&sup2; = 0 is the statement that <strong>a boundary has no boundary</strong>.
      The edge of a disc is a circle; the circle has no edge. That is also why Stokes' theorem is true in
      the form</p>
    <p class="formula" style="text-align:center">&int;<sub>M</sub> d&omega; =
      &int;<sub>&part;M</sub> &omega;</p>
    <p class="lede">The sum of the change throughout a region equals what is recorded on its edge.
      Interior and boundary carry the same information &mdash; which is this framework's
      <a href="/moebius/">seam</a>, written as a theorem rather than a picture.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">&#10022;</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">&mdash; and the philosophy, kept separate from it</p>
    <h2 id="t-dx-philosophy">What dx Is, in the Framework</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">The mathematics above is not in dispute and the framework claims no part of it.
      What follows is a reading laid over it: three commitments, each of which buys something and costs
      something.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <div class="frame">
      <h3 id="t-dx-commit-one">First: the gathering is prior to the quantity</h3>
      <p>Standard practice treats a quantity as primary and integration as an operation performed on it.
        The framework inverts that. <strong>An amount is what a completed gathering leaves behind</strong>
        &mdash; the noun is the residue of the verb, not the other way round. On this reading
        <span class="formula-inline">&int;</span> is not a procedure applied to a world of quantities;
        quantities are what you have once an &int; has finished.</p>
      <p>This is why the chapter insists on reading
        <span class="formula-inline">&int;<sub>x</sub><sup>x+dx</sup> f = f(x) dx</span> as a definition
        rather than an approximation. A differential is not a small <em>amount</em>. It is the smallest
        <em>act of gathering that has been carried through</em> &mdash; the least sum that is still a
        sum. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
      <p class="muted">What it buys: a self can be made of these, because an act is the kind of thing a
        moment of experience could be and a quantity is not. What it costs: nothing in the mathematics
        requires this ordering. Both directions are consistent, and the framework is choosing the one it
        can build a mind out of.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <h3 id="t-dx-commit-two">Second: completion, not vanishing</h3>
    <p>Berkeley's objection bites on the idea that dx is <em>on its way</em> to nothing &mdash; a quantity
      caught mid-disappearance, which is why it can be both discarded and divided by. The framework
      refuses that picture entirely. A differential is not vanishing. It is <strong>finished</strong>.</p>
    <p>And there is a reason this is not merely a change of tone. If a differential is a completed act,
      then it has no interior left to resolve &mdash; which is precisely the content of
      <span class="formula-inline">d&sup2; = 0</span>. Something whose second-order structure is exactly
      zero is not an object that has been cut off early; it is an object with nothing further inside it.
      The framework reads the nilpotence of d as the mathematical signature of completeness rather than
      of smallness. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="muted">The caution, stated plainly: d&sup2; = 0 is a theorem about an operator in exterior
      calculus and an axiom about a ring element in synthetic geometry, and in neither case does it mean
      what &ldquo;complete&rdquo; means here. The resonance is real and the identification is the
      framework's own. It is a reading of a theorem, not a consequence of one.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <h3 id="t-dx-commit-three">Third: the grain is given, not chosen</h3>
    <p>The third commitment is the one that makes the rest of this page possible. In mathematics, dx is
      <em>arbitrarily</em> small &mdash; the whole content of the limit is that no particular scale is
      privileged. In the framework it is not arbitrary. <strong>A physical system integrates at the
      finest grain its substrate can actually complete a gather at</strong>, and that grain is set by
      thermodynamics rather than preference: Landauer's bound fixes the minimum energy to erase one bit
      at k<sub>B</sub>T&thinsp;ln&thinsp;2, about 2.9 &times; 10<sup>&minus;21</sup> J at 300 K, measured
      directly in 2012. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>So a mind does not choose its dx. It is issued one by its body, and the thresholds further down
      this page &mdash; the millisecond of a spike, the hundreds of milliseconds of a perceptual moment,
      the day of a circadian cycle &mdash; are the grains at which the gathering can actually be carried
      through. Awareness appears at particular grains because gathering is a physical act with a price.
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="lede">Put the three together and the position is simple to state and easy to attack.
      <em>Gathering is prior to amount; a differential is a gathering that has been completed, not one
      that is disappearing; and which gatherings can be completed is decided by the world, not by the
      mathematics.</em></p>
    <p class="muted">The strongest objection remains the one in the opening frame and it is not answered
      anywhere on this page: analysis does not need any of this, and a reading that is optional is a
      reading that has to earn its keep elsewhere. It earns it, if at all, in what it lets
      <a href="/the-self/">the self</a> and <a href="/skeletal-splice/#t-emergence">the accumulation</a>
      be &mdash; not here.</p>
  </div>
</section>

