<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <h2 id="t-threshold">Thresholds: why awareness appears at particular grains</h2>
    <p class="lede">If every gather were equivalent, nothing would ever happen at a particular scale. But
      things plainly do happen at particular scales, and only there.</p>
    <p>Water does not become wet gradually as molecules are added; wetness is a property of the bulk, and
      it arrives. A neuron does not fire a little more as charge accumulates; it holds and then goes. A
      melody is not in any note. In every case the system integrates over some window, and when the window
      is right the aggregate acquires a property that none of the slivers had.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>The framework's reading: those windows are <strong>grains</strong> &mdash; particular values of
      <span class="formula-inline">dx</span> at which the gathering closes on itself. Below the grain the
      system is summing. At the grain the sum becomes a term in a further sum, and the system has, for the
      first time, something to be aware <em>with</em>. Awareness on this account is not a substance added
      to matter; it is what a gather is, from the inside, once the gather is fine enough to include its own
      gathering. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>This is why the page sits in Part&nbsp;II rather than in the physics. It is a reading of
      <a href="/the-self/#t-dx">the integral self</a> pushed down to its smallest term: a moment of
      experience is one <span class="formula-inline">dx</span> of a life; a life is the integral; and the
      reason a moment feels like <em>something</em> rather than nothing is that even the smallest term is
      already a completed sum.</p>
    <h3 id="t-ladder">The grains the framework names</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The physical grain</strong> &mdash; below which the question of further division stops
        being meaningful. Physics has a candidate, the Planck length, and is careful to say it is a scale
        at which current theory fails rather than a proven floor. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
      <li><strong>The dynamical grain</strong> &mdash; the window over which a system's own feedback closes:
        a few milliseconds in a neuron, tens in a cortical loop, a season in a forest.
        <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
      <li><strong>The reflexive grain</strong> &mdash; where the gather includes a model of the gatherer.
        This is where the framework places awareness, and where it has no measurement to offer.
        <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></li>
      <li><strong>The ground</strong> &mdash; nought, never reached, the floor that no radius arrives at.
        See <a href="/spiral/#t-placed">the placed integers</a>. <span class="myth">&#9670;</span></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <h2 id="t-transfer">The transfer: awareness is paid for out of memory</h2>
    <p class="lede">Here is the part of the chapter that is a genuine proposal rather than a restatement.
      Emergence is not free. When a new awareness comes up at a grain, information moves &mdash; and the
      place it moves <em>from</em> is the record.</p>
    <p>Put plainly: the substrate holds a stored past. Call its information content
      <span class="formula-inline">M</span>. The emergent awareness holds a present model. Call its content
      <span class="formula-inline">A</span>. The framework's claim is that the two are drawn on one
      account.</p>
    <p class="formula" style="text-align:center">&Delta;A = &minus;&eta;&thinsp;&Delta;M, &nbsp; 0 &lt;
      &eta; &le; 1 &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; so that &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; A + M/&eta; = constant</p>
    <p>Every bit the awareness acquires is a bit the record loses, discounted by an efficiency
      <span class="formula-inline">&eta;</span> that is never better than one and in practice much worse.
      Awareness, on this reading, is <strong>the record being spent</strong>. A system that becomes more
      present becomes, by exactly that much, less faithful to what it was.
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>

    <h3 id="t-why">Why it would have to be that way</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Because the sliver is already complete.</strong> If <span class="formula-inline">dx</span>
        is a finished sum, it cannot be added to the ledger for free; it has to be <em>composed</em> of
        terms that were somewhere before. The new awareness is not conjured out of the substrate's spare
        capacity, because the framework does not grant the substrate any spare capacity: everything it has
        is already the record of what it has been.</li>
      <li><strong>Because erasure has a price, and that price is real.</strong> Landauer showed in 1961 that
        erasing one bit of information must dissipate at least
        <span class="formula-inline">k<sub>B</sub>T&thinsp;ln&thinsp;2</span> &mdash; about
        2.9&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;10<sup>&minus;21</sup>&nbsp;J at room temperature &mdash; and the bound has
        been measured, in colloidal and nanomagnetic systems, since 2012. Information and thermodynamics
        are the same ledger. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> What the framework adds is the direction of
        the flow: the erasure is not waste heat from an unrelated computation, it is the <em>payment</em>.
        <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></li>
      <li><strong>Because the one place we can look already behaves like this.</strong> Brains do not
        accumulate; they overwrite. The evidence is set out in the next panel.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3 id="t-reconsolidation">Recall degrades the thing recalled</h3>
      <p>A consolidated memory, when retrieved, becomes labile again and must be re-stored. Nader, Schafe
        and LeDoux (<em>Nature</em>, 2000) blocked protein synthesis in the amygdala <em>after</em>
        retrieval and the fear memory did not survive &mdash; an already-consolidated trace was lost by the
        act of being remembered. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <p>Every recollection is therefore a re-writing, and re-writing is lossy. The framework reads this as
        the transfer at human scale: the vividness of the present act of remembering is bought with the
        fidelity of the record. You can have the past, or you can have it <em>now</em>, and the exchange
        rate is <span class="formula-inline">&eta;</span>. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3 id="t-downscaling">Sleep pays the bill</h3>
      <p>The synaptic homeostasis hypothesis (Tononi &amp; Cirelli) holds that waking experience drives net
        synaptic potentiation, which is unsustainable in energy and in signal-to-noise, and that sleep
        renormalises it &mdash; weakening broadly so that the strongest traces survive in relief.
        <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> It is contested in its strong form, and the clearance literature
        argued over on <a href="/stress-tests/">the stress-tests page</a> is contested too.</p>
      <p>The shape is still the one this chapter predicts: a day of awareness incurs a debt, and the debt is
        settled by degrading the record &mdash; not at random, but by preferentially keeping what the
        awareness used. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

