<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <h2 id="t-objections">What would break it</h2>
    <p class="lede">A proposal that cannot be broken is not a proposal. Three things would break this one.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A lossless gain.</strong> A system that measurably increases its self-model without any
        measurable degradation of its stored past &mdash; and crucially, without having simply imported the
        information from outside. Note the escape hatch this leaves, and how large it is: almost any
        counter-example can be re-described as import. The chapter is weaker for that, and it is said here
        rather than left for a reader to notice.</li>
      <li><strong>An established floor with nothing at it.</strong> If physics settles on a smallest length
        and nothing whatever changes in kind there, the identification of grain with threshold loses its
        best analogy.</li>
      <li><strong>The arrow argument.</strong> The transfer described here runs one way, and so quietly
        assumes the asymmetry the framework is elsewhere trying to <em>derive</em>. That is close to
        circular, it is the same weak joint confessed at
        <a href="/stress-tests/#t-entropy">the entropy objection</a>, and the attempt to derive it has
        now been run and has <a href="/stress-tests/#t-toy">failed</a>. Treat the one-way transfer as
        assumed, not earned.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="muted">And one thing that would <em>not</em> break it, though it is often offered as if it
      would: the fact that <span class="formula-inline">dx</span> can be eliminated from analysis
      altogether. Of course it can. The question this page asks is not whether the formalism needs
      infinitesimals, but whether the world is the kind of thing that is assembled out of completed smallest
      acts. Nothing in the epsilon-delta definition answers that either way.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <h2 id="t-ledger">Earned, chosen, refused</h2>
    <dl class="glossary">
      <dt>Earned</dt>
      <dd>The calculus is exact and is used exactly. Landauer's bound is a theorem of statistical mechanics
        and has been measured. Reconsolidation is a replicated experimental finding. Emergent properties at
        characteristic scales are ordinary physics, not mysticism.</dd>
      <dt>Chosen</dt>
      <dd>Reading <span class="formula-inline">dx</span> as the smallest <em>completed</em> integral rather
        than a limit artefact. Identifying a grain of gathering with a threshold of awareness. Writing the
        exchange as <span class="formula-inline">&Delta;A = &minus;&eta;&thinsp;&Delta;M</span> when nothing
        yet measures <span class="formula-inline">&eta;</span>.</dd>
      <dt>Refused</dt>
      <dd>The claim that this explains consciousness; it proposes a price, not a mechanism. The claim that
        Landauer's bound is evidence <em>for</em> the transfer rather than the reason a transfer would have
        to cost something. And the move of treating every counter-example as imported information, which is
        named above as the chapter's cheapest escape.</dd>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <h2 id="t-glossary">Terms</h2>
    <dl class="glossary">
      <dt>Differential (dx)</dt>
      <dd>In standard analysis, a formal object inside an integral; here, the smallest finished gather.</dd>
      <dt>Grain</dt>
      <dd>A value of <span class="formula-inline">dx</span> at which a gathering closes on itself and the
        aggregate acquires a property no sliver had.</dd>
      <dt>Transfer</dt>
      <dd>The movement of information out of the stored record and into an emergent awareness.</dd>
      <dt>&eta; (eta)</dt>
      <dd>The efficiency of that transfer. At most one; unmeasured; probably small.</dd>
      <dt>Landauer's bound</dt>
      <dd>The minimum energy dissipated by erasing one bit of information:
        <span class="formula-inline">k<sub>B</sub>T&thinsp;ln&thinsp;2</span>.</dd>
    </dl>
    <h3>Reading</h3>
    <ul class="biblio">
      <li>Robinson, A. — <em>Non-standard Analysis</em> (1966). Infinitesimals made rigorous, with no
        metaphysics attached.</li>
      <li>Landauer, R. — &ldquo;Irreversibility and Heat Generation in the Computing Process&rdquo;,
        <em>IBM J. Res. Dev.</em> <strong>5</strong> (1961).</li>
      <li>Bérut, A. <em>et al.</em> — &ldquo;Experimental verification of Landauer's principle&rdquo;,
        <em>Nature</em> <strong>483</strong> (2012).</li>
      <li>Nader, K., Schafe, G. &amp; LeDoux, J. — &ldquo;Fear memories require protein synthesis in the
        amygdala for reconsolidation after retrieval&rdquo;, <em>Nature</em> <strong>406</strong> (2000).</li>
      <li>Tononi, G. &amp; Cirelli, C. — &ldquo;Sleep and the price of plasticity&rdquo;, <em>Neuron</em>
        <strong>81</strong> (2014).</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

